 ("cond", "Conductivity is a measure of the ability of a substance to allow electron flow.", CT, 2),
 ("corr", "Corrosion is the deterioration of a material due to interaction with its environment.", CT, 2),
 ("elys", "Electrolysis is the decomposition by electric current", CT, 2),
 ("water", "This chapter will concentrate on the corrosion of metals in water-based environments (electrolytes).", CT, 2),
 ("echem", "Corrosion is electrochemical in nature because the corrosive chemical reactions involve transfer of charge.", CT, 3),
 ("ox1", "The process in which electrons are given up and positive metal ions are formed is called oxidation.", CT, 3),
 ("path", "In order for corrosion to occur, the micro-cells must also be connected through some conducting path external to the metal.", CT, 4),
 ("oxred", "Oxidation is the process of losing electrons; reduction is the process of gaining electrons.", CT, 5),
 ("anode", "The metal atoms release electrons (are oxidized) and become positive ions. The site at which this occurs is known as the anode.", CT, 5),
 ("simul", "An oxidation process cannot take place without a simultaneous reduction (gain of electrons) process.", CT, 5),
 ("gen", "General corrosion is the process whereby the surface of a metal undergoes a slow, relatively uniform; removal of material.", CT, 6),
 ("pass", "Passivity is the characteristic of a metal exhibited when that metal does not become active in the corrosion reaction.", CT, 6),
 ("film", "Passivity is caused by the buildup of a stable, tenacious layer of metal oxide on the surface of the metal.", CT, 6),
 ("pol", "In electrochemical cells, the decrease in cell potential caused by the operation of the cell (current flow) is called polarization.", CT, 7),
 ("both", "Both concentration and activation polarization decrease the net oxidation-reduction reaction rate.", CT, 9),
 ("two", "The two conditions typically required for a metal to undergo general corrosion are: 1) metal and water in the same environment, and 2) a chemical reaction between the metal and water that forms an oxide.", GC, 11),
 ("barrier", "the oxide layer represents a barrier to continued corrosion and tends to slow the corrosion rate.", GC, 13),
 ("rate", "A rule of thumb is that the reaction rate doubles with a 20", GC, 13),
 ("vel", "Water velocities of 30 to 40 ft per second are usually considered to cause erosion.", GC, 13),
 ("o2", "The presence of oxygen in water to which iron is exposed increases the corrosion rate.", GC, 13),
 ("dep", "that remove the absorbed atomic hydrogen are called depolarizers.", GC, 14),
 ("surf", "Deposits, scale, or irregular surfaces create areas on the metal where local corrosion can initiate and proceed at a faster rate than normal.", GC, 16),
 ("pick", "This process, referred to as pretreatment, or pickling, involves careful control of reactor coolant water chemistry and temperature during the pretreatment period.", GC, 16),
